= Solution 1: CDO Basics
<solution-1-cdo-basics>
== Task 1: Install and Verify CDO
<task-1-install-and-verify-cdo>
=== Command to check version:
<command-to-check-version>
```bash
cdo --version
```

=== Example output:
<example-output>
```
Climate Data Operators version 2.4.0 (https://mpimet.mpg.de/cdo)
Copyright: (C) 2005-2024 Max Planck Institute for Meteorology
Based on: NetCDF 4.9.2 | HDF5 1.14.3 | JasPer 4.0.0 | ...
Features: OpenMP 16 threads, HDF5, NC4/HDF5, SZIP, ZLIB, S3
```

=== Answers:
<answers>
+ #strong[What command shows the installed CDO version?] `cdo --version`
  or `cdo -V`
+ #strong[What is your installed CDO version?] (Will vary; example:
  2.4.0)
+ #strong[What is the installed NetCDF library version?] Look at the
  "Based on" line in `cdo --version` output.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 2: Explore CDO Operators
<task-2-explore-cdo-operators>
=== Example table of 10 operators:
<example-table-of-10-operators>
#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (8.33%, 27.78%, 27.78%, 36.11%),
    align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,),
    table.header([\#], [Operator], [Category], [Description],),
    table.hline(),
    [1], [`sinfo`], [INFO], [Prints information about a dataset],
    [2], [`copy`], [FILE OPERATIONS], [Copies a dataset (can also
    compress)],
    [3], [`selyear`], [SELECT], [Selects a specific time period by
    year],
    [4], [`mergetime`], [FILE OPERATIONS], [Merges multiple files along
    the time axis],
    [5], [`monmean`], [STATISTICS], [Computes monthly mean values],
    [6], [`yearmean`], [STATISTICS], [Computes annual mean values],
    [7], [`fldmean`], [STATISTICS], [Computes spatial (field) mean],
    [8], [`remapbil`], [REMAPPING], [Bilinear interpolation to a target
    grid],
    [9], [`sub`], [ARITHMETIC], [Subtracts two fields (field1 -
    field2)],
    [10], [`outputts`], [OUTPUT], [Prints time series as ASCII table],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 3: Run `sinfo` on a Sample File
<task-3-run-sinfo-on-a-sample-file>
=== Command:
<command>
```bash
cdo sinfo ../datasets/tas_sample.nc > sinfo_output.txt
```

=== Example output:
<example-output-1>
```
   File format: NetCDF4
    -1 : Institut Source   Steptype Levels Num    Points Num Dtype : Parameter name
     1 : unknown/mixed     instant      1   1     64800   1  F32  : tas
   Grid coord : lonlat
   Grid size  : 64800 (360 x 180)
   Grid origin: -179.5 / -89.5
   Grid inc   : 1 / 1
   Vertical coordinates : 0 Pa
   Time range : 372 steps
   Time range : 1980-01-01 00:00:00 - 2010-12-01 00:00:00
   Calendar   : standard
   ...
```

=== Answers:
<answers-1>
+ #strong[How many variables?] 1 variable: `tas`
+ #strong[Grid type and dimensions?] lonlat (regular
  latitude-longitude), 360×180 cells (64800 points)
+ #strong[Time range?] 1980-01-01 to 2010-12-01 (372 months, 31 years)
+ #strong[How many time steps?] 372
+ #strong[Calendar type?] standard (Gregorian)
+ #strong[Data type?] F32 (32-bit float)

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Task 4: CDO Architecture Questions
<task-4-cdo-architecture-questions>
=== 1. What does CDO stand for?
<what-does-cdo-stand-for>
CDO = #strong[Climate Data Operators];. It is a command-line tool for
manipulating and analyzing climate and forecast data (NetCDF, GRIB,
etc.).

=== 2. How does CDO handle memory for large datasets?
<how-does-cdo-handle-memory-for-large-datasets>
CDO uses a stream-based processing model. It reads data in chunks
(tiles) and processes them without loading the entire dataset into
memory. The `-buffersize` option controls the chunk size. For very large
datasets, CDO can use the `-t` (tile) option to process data in spatial
tiles, and can work with external memory (file-based temporary storage).

=== 3. File formats CDO can read/write (5+ formats):
<file-formats-cdo-can-readwrite-5-formats>
- NetCDF3/4 (.nc)
- GRIB1/GRIB2 (.grb, .grib)
- SERVICE (DAP/OPeNDAP URLs)
- EXTRA (various binary formats)
- IEG (IEEE binary)
- CSV / ASCII table
- GRB (GRIB)
- HDF5 (.h5, via NetCDF4)

=== 4. Difference between operator and modifier:
<difference-between-operator-and-modifier>
- #strong[Operator];: Performs an action on the data (e.g., `monmean`,
  `copy`, `selname`)
- #strong[Modifier];: Changes how operators behave (e.g., `-f nc4`
  changes output format, `-z zip` compresses, `-selname` restricts which
  variables are processed). Modifiers are prefixed with `-` and can be
  combined.

=== 5. The pipe (`-`) meaning:
<the-pipe---meaning>
The pipe character `-` tells CDO to read from stdin or write to stdout.
This enables chaining multiple CDO commands without intermediate files:

```bash
cdo monmean -selyear,2000/2010 input.nc - | cdo yearmean - output.nc
```

This is useful because it avoids writing temporary files to disk, saving
I/O and disk space.

=== 6. Purpose of `--no_metadata`:
<purpose-of---no_metadata>
The `--no_metadata` option suppresses the output of metadata in ASCII
output commands like `outputtab`, `outputts`, etc. It produces cleaner,
machine-readable tables without header lines.

#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)

== Bonus Challenge
<bonus-challenge>
=== Command:
<command-1>
```bash
cdo yearmean -fldmean -selyear,2000/2005 ../datasets/tas_sample.nc output.nc
```

=== Explanation (right to left):
<explanation-right-to-left>
+ `-selyear,2000/2005` selects time steps from years 2000 to 2005
+ `-fldmean` computes the spatial (field) mean at each time step →
  global mean time series
+ `cdo yearmean` computes the annual mean of the global mean time series
+ Result: one value per year (2000--2005) of global mean temperature
